Guest-compatible stress-ng builds
=================================

This contributor tool builds stress-ng 0.19.02 on the host for the Debian 12
amd64 noise guests provisioned by scripts/create_vm.sh. Debian 12's packaged
0.15.06 lacks the scheduler workload used by scripts/evaluate.sh.

One completed build can be distributed to multiple noise VMs. No compiler
or container runtime is needed in the guests. VM creation does not depend
on this tool; it remains separate from the driver, BPF and libgomp builds.


Build on the host
-----------------

Requires Docker on an x86_64 Linux host. Run from the project root, stopping
on any error (set -e). Use sudo docker if daemon access requires it. In shell
assignments, sudo belongs inside the command substitution:

    docker build --platform linux/amd64 \
        -t phantom-stress-ng:0.19.02-debian12 dev-tools/stress-ng
    BUILD_IMAGE=$(docker image inspect --format '{{.Id}}' phantom-stress-ng:0.19.02-debian12)

For sudo users, the second line is:

    BUILD_IMAGE=$(sudo docker image inspect --format '{{.Id}}' phantom-stress-ng:0.19.02-debian12)

Choose a new, empty build directory outside the repository. Each invocation
uses a fresh source copy and refuses to reuse build output:

    STRESS_BUILD="$HOME/src/build/stress-ng-0.19.02"
    mkdir -p "$STRESS_BUILD"
    docker run --rm --platform linux/amd64 \
        --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)" \
        --mount "type=bind,src=$STRESS_BUILD,dst=/build" \
        --env BUILD_JOBS="$(nproc)" \
        "$BUILD_IMAGE"
    printf '%s\n' "$BUILD_IMAGE" > "$STRESS_BUILD/release/container-image.txt"

BUILD_JOBS uses all processing units available to the host shell. The image
contains the checksum-verified upstream source; docker run needs no network.
The build uses Debian's native compiler, a generic x86-64 CPU target, fixed
container paths, C locale, UTC, and the source archive's timestamp through
SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH. It does not build GCC.

STATIC=1 produces a binary without shared-library dependencies. The builder
checks that property and runs a one-second, one-worker smoke test with the
same scheduler settings used by evaluate.sh before publishing release/.
Optional stressors requiring libraries absent from the build image may be
unavailable; this artifact is intended for the scheduler workload.

release/ contains the binary, its SHA256SUMS, license, source checksum,
compiler/package and configuration records, version, and smoke-test output.
Full source, objects and build.log remain in STRESS_BUILD outside the repo.


Publish and use in noise guests
-------------------------------

Publish the completed release to a new directory in the noise share:

    STRESS_RELEASE="$PWD/hg_noise_shared_dir/stress-ng/0.19.02"
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$STRESS_RELEASE")"
    mkdir "$STRESS_RELEASE"
    cp -a "$STRESS_BUILD/release/." "$STRESS_RELEASE/"

Export ./hg_noise_shared_dir in each noise VM with --mode=dev and
--shared-dir=./hg_noise_shared_dir when launching from the project root
(../hg_noise_shared_dir from scripts/). Sharing is read-only by default.
Follow the sharing guide at ../../Documentation/host-guest-sharing.md to mount hostshare at /mnt/hostshare inside each VM.
The container does not need to stay running.

Inside each noise VM, copy the release onto its own disk and verify it:

    GUEST_STRESS="$HOME/stress-ng-releases/0.19.02"
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$GUEST_STRESS")"
    mkdir "$GUEST_STRESS"
    cp -a --no-preserve=ownership /mnt/hostshare/stress-ng/0.19.02/. "$GUEST_STRESS/"
    (cd "$GUEST_STRESS" && sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS)
    "$GUEST_STRESS/stress-ng" --version
    sudo install -m 0755 "$GUEST_STRESS/stress-ng" /usr/local/bin/stress-ng
    hash -r
    command -v stress-ng
    sudo sh -c 'command -v stress-ng; stress-ng --version'

Both lookups must select /usr/local/bin/stress-ng. This leaves the distro's
/usr/bin/stress-ng intact. evaluate.sh uses the newer binary via PATH and
checks the required options before starting a batch. Use identical release
hashes and noise parameters across default, passive and pvsched batches.
Run from the guest disk; 9p is only the distribution channel.


Reproducing a build
-------------------

The release version and archive SHA-256 are fixed in Dockerfile. Retain the
built image by ID (for example, docker image save), build output and release
metadata. BASE_IMAGE can be overridden with a digest-pinned Debian 12 image.
The default base tag and apt repositories can change, so rebuilding the
Dockerfile later does not itself freeze compiler/package versions. Reuse
the saved build image to preserve those inputs. Byte-for-byte reproducibility
requires a second clean build and comparison of release/SHA256SUMS.

The static binary still requires compatible x86-64 Linux kernel facilities;
validate the scheduler workload in each guest before collecting results.

Upstream build instructions and static build support:
https://github.com/ColinIanKing/stress-ng/blob/V0.19.02/README.md
